**Goal:** move a lifecycle out of boolean flags and into named states, with side effects in declared actors.

A machine is a definition of named states and the events that move between them. Use an XState machine for the application's named states, retries, cancellation, or a lifecycle you need to resume or inspect.
Model the states and the events that move between them instead of accumulating booleans whose combinations have no meaning.

An actor is a running unit that performs declared work.
A side effect is work outside the state description, such as reading a file.
A [Scope](/lore/scopes-own-resources) owns cleanup when that work ends.

## Worked example: inspecting a file

`packages/core/src/inspect-machine.ts` is the working example.
It reads a file and ends in one of two final states.

### 1. Declare the side effect as an actor

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/inspect-machine.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=27-31 title="readStats is the only code that touches the file"

```

`createEffectLogic` defines an Effect-backed side effect with an input [Schema](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary).

### 2. Let the machine choose transitions

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/inspect-machine.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=41-64 {45-47,52,58} title="reading invokes readStats, then lands in a final state"

```

`setupEffect` declares actors. The machine invokes `readStats` by name in the `reading` state.
The machine chooses transitions and records its outcome. The invoked actor performs the file read.

A read failure lands in `unreadable` with its cause. The failure is a state you can test.

### 3. Start it inside Effect and wait for the outcome

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/inspect-machine.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=67-70 {69-70} title="createEffectActor starts it; watchActor reports it"

```

`createEffectActor` starts the machine inside Effect's scope. `watchActor` makes the run visible to devtools.
`join` waits for the final outcome. `Effect.orDie` treats a machine-level failure as a programming error. The domain read failure remains an explicit machine state.

## Why a machine

Two booleans, `loading` and `failed`, allow four combinations. Only three of them mean anything here.
This machine has exactly three states: `reading`, `inspected`, and `unreadable`.

The [fence](/lore/the-fence) keeps side effects in declared actors. `oxlint.config.ts` loads the lint plugin that `@xstate/effect` ships and sets `xstate-effect/no-inline-effect` to `error`. That rule catches inline Effects in machine actions and actors.

The stack pins XState `6.0.0-beta.1` and `@xstate/effect` `1.0.0-beta.0` together.
The [migration note](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/.brain/archives/resources/xstate-alpha69-migration.svx#beta1-and-bridge-beta0) records the beta API changes and persistence audit.

## Next

Use [the add-a-lifecycle-machine skill](/skills/add-a-lifecycle-machine) to build one.

See [one capability, every surface](/lore/one-capability-every-surface) for how a capability can own a machine and [the fence](/lore/the-fence) for the rule that protects it.

A machine in memory ends with its process. [Restart must reconstruct known state](/lore/restart-from-known-state) and [supervision chooses what restarts](/lore/supervision-is-a-recovery-policy) cover recovery.
